            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-119375-libsqlite3-0",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-119375 medium vulnerability for libsqlite3-0 package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "SQLite before 3.54.0 contains an information disclosure vulnerability that allows attackers to read uninitialized page-cache memory by supplying a WAL file with an undersized page size. Attackers can craft a database and WAL file whose oversized record payload makes queries like substr() return stale bytes, disclosing other table rows and internal pointers."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-119375",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-119375\nSeverity: medium\nPackage: libsqlite3-0\nVersion: 3.46.1-7+deb13u2\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /var/lib/dpkg/status\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:13\nLink: [CVE-2026-119375](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-119375)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-119375**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| medium  | libsqlite3-0  | 3.46.1-7+deb13u2  |   | deb  | /var/lib/dpkg/status  | debian:distro:debian:13  | [CVE-2026-119375](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-119375)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/libsqlite3-0@3.46.1-7%2Bdeb13u2?arch=arm64\u0026distro=debian-13.7\u0026upstream=sqlite3"
                ],
                "security-severity": "6.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-119371-libsqlite3-0",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-119371 medium vulnerability for libsqlite3-0 package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "SQLite before 3.54.0 contains a stack exhaustion vulnerability that allows attackers to bypass the SQLITE_LIMIT_EXPR_DEPTH nesting limit because common table expressions are not counted against it. Attackers can supply a WITH clause chaining tens of thousands of CTEs, via plain SQL or a view in an untrusted database file, causing unbounded selectExpander() recursion that crashes the process."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-119371",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-119371\nSeverity: medium\nPackage: libsqlite3-0\nVersion: 3.46.1-7+deb13u2\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /var/lib/dpkg/status\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:13\nLink: [CVE-2026-119371](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-119371)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-119371**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| medium  | libsqlite3-0  | 3.46.1-7+deb13u2  |   | deb  | /var/lib/dpkg/status  | debian:distro:debian:13  | [CVE-2026-119371](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-119371)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/libsqlite3-0@3.46.1-7%2Bdeb13u2?arch=arm64\u0026distro=debian-13.7\u0026upstream=sqlite3"
                ],
                "security-severity": "6.8"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-100290-libc-bin",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-100290 low vulnerability for libc-bin package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The regcomp and regexec functions in the GNU C Library version 2.3 to version 2.44 may read freed memory and free the same heap block twice when a memory allocation fails while a regular expression is compiled or matched.  The re_dfa_add_node (the NFA node table) and build_range_exp (bracket ranges) grow several arrays with consecutive realloc calls and store the new pointers only after all of them succeed.  When a later call fails, the pattern buffer keeps pointers to blocks that were already freed, and the error cleanup frees them again.  In regexec, the back-reference cache (match_ctx_add_entry) frees its array when a realloc fails but keeps the pointer, which is freed again on the error path.  An attacker does not need to supply a crafted regular expression: patterns large enough to grow these arrays are common.  The failure has to hit a specific allocation, though, so the issue can only be reached when the system is already under memory pressure.  An attacker who supplies the pattern or the subject string has little control over which allocation fails, so the expected impact is a crash of the process.  This issue affects all architectures and ABIs supported by the GNU C Library."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-100290",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-100290\nSeverity: low\nPackage: libc-bin\nVersion: 2.41-12+deb13u4\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /var/lib/dpkg/status\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:13\nLink: [CVE-2026-100290](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-100290)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-100290**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| low  | libc-bin  | 2.41-12+deb13u4  |   | deb  | /var/lib/dpkg/status  | debian:distro:debian:13  | [CVE-2026-100290](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-100290)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/libc-bin@2.41-12%2Bdeb13u4?arch=arm64\u0026distro=debian-13.7\u0026upstream=glibc"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.7"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-100290-libc6",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-100290 low vulnerability for libc6 package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The regcomp and regexec functions in the GNU C Library version 2.3 to version 2.44 may read freed memory and free the same heap block twice when a memory allocation fails while a regular expression is compiled or matched.  The re_dfa_add_node (the NFA node table) and build_range_exp (bracket ranges) grow several arrays with consecutive realloc calls and store the new pointers only after all of them succeed.  When a later call fails, the pattern buffer keeps pointers to blocks that were already freed, and the error cleanup frees them again.  In regexec, the back-reference cache (match_ctx_add_entry) frees its array when a realloc fails but keeps the pointer, which is freed again on the error path.  An attacker does not need to supply a crafted regular expression: patterns large enough to grow these arrays are common.  The failure has to hit a specific allocation, though, so the issue can only be reached when the system is already under memory pressure.  An attacker who supplies the pattern or the subject string has little control over which allocation fails, so the expected impact is a crash of the process.  This issue affects all architectures and ABIs supported by the GNU C Library."
              },
