            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-54370-libacl1",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-54370 high vulnerability for libacl1 package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "acl before version 2.4.0 contains a time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU) race condition vulnerability that allows local attackers to escalate privileges by replacing a pathname component with a symbolic link between an lstat() check and subsequent symlink-following operations such as stat(), chown(), chmod(), acl_get_file(), and acl_set_file(). Attackers who control a pathname component can redirect file access control list operations to arbitrary files when getfacl, setfacl, or chacl is invoked by a privileged process over an attacker-controlled path, resulting in local privilege escalation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-54370",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-54370\nSeverity: high\nPackage: libacl1\nVersion: 2.3.2-2+b1\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /var/lib/dpkg/status\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:13\nLink: [CVE-2026-54370](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-54370)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-54370**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| high  | libacl1  | 2.3.2-2+b1  |   | deb  | /var/lib/dpkg/status  | debian:distro:debian:13  | [CVE-2026-54370](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-54370)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/libacl1@2.3.2-2%2Bb1?arch=arm64\u0026distro=debian-13.7\u0026upstream=acl%402.3.2-2"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.2"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-107496-libssl3t64",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-107496 low vulnerability for libssl3t64 package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Issue summary: Encrypting a CMS message to an EC certificate whose group order is 1 may cause CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() to loop indefinitely.  Impact summary: An attacker able to supply the recipient certificate to an application performing CMS encryption can cause that application to loop indefinitely, consuming CPU and resulting in Denial of Service.  CWE: CWE-835: Loop with Unreachable Exit Condition ('Infinite Loop')  Description: CMS_encrypt() and CMS_encrypt_ex() encrypt a message to one or more recipient certificates and, for an EC recipient, generate an ephemeral key in the recipient's group. If the certificate declares a group order of 1, random scalar generation can only produce zero. The key generation routine then retries without limit while waiting for a nonzero private scalar, so the call never returns.  The OpenSSL TLS and QUIC implementations do not use this path. An application is exposed only if it calls CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() with a recipient certificate whose EC parameters are attacker-controlled. Certificates with normal EC group orders do not trigger the loop.  OpenSSL 4.0 and later reject unknown explicit EC groups in default builds. These versions are affected only when configured with the enable-ec_explicit_curves option.  The result is a hang that consumes a CPU core until the process is interrupted, a Denial of Service. The severity is Low because the attacker must supply the recipient certificate and the application must choose to encrypt to it; ordinary CMS encryption to trusted recipients is unaffected.  The fix makes EC key generation fail when the group has no valid nonzero private scalar, instead of retrying indefinitely.  FIPS impact: no The FIPS modules in 4.1, 4.0, 3.5 and 3.0 are not affected. The FIPS provider does not support arbitrary explicit EC domain parameters, so an order-1 group is rejected during key import before the vulnerable key generation path is reached."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-107496",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-107496\nSeverity: low\nPackage: libssl3t64\nVersion: 3.5.7-1~deb13u3\nFix Version: \nType: deb\nLocation: /var/lib/dpkg/status\nData Namespace: debian:distro:debian:13\nLink: [CVE-2026-107496](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-107496)",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-107496**\n| Severity | Package | Version | Fix Version | Type | Location | Data Namespace | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| low  | libssl3t64  | 3.5.7-1~deb13u3  |   | deb  | /var/lib/dpkg/status  | debian:distro:debian:13  | [CVE-2026-107496](https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-107496)  |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/libssl3t64@3.5.7-1~deb13u3?arch=arm64\u0026distro=debian-13.7\u0026upstream=openssl"
                ],
                "security-severity": "0.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-107496-openssl-provider-legacy",
              "name": "DpkgMatcherExactIndirectMatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-107496 low vulnerability for openssl-provider-legacy package"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Issue summary: Encrypting a CMS message to an EC certificate whose group order is 1 may cause CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() to loop indefinitely.  Impact summary: An attacker able to supply the recipient certificate to an application performing CMS encryption can cause that application to loop indefinitely, consuming CPU and resulting in Denial of Service.  CWE: CWE-835: Loop with Unreachable Exit Condition ('Infinite Loop')  Description: CMS_encrypt() and CMS_encrypt_ex() encrypt a message to one or more recipient certificates and, for an EC recipient, generate an ephemeral key in the recipient's group. If the certificate declares a group order of 1, random scalar generation can only produce zero. The key generation routine then retries without limit while waiting for a nonzero private scalar, so the call never returns.  The OpenSSL TLS and QUIC implementations do not use this path. An application is exposed only if it calls CMS_encrypt() or CMS_encrypt_ex() with a recipient certificate whose EC parameters are attacker-controlled. Certificates with normal EC group orders do not trigger the loop.  OpenSSL 4.0 and later reject unknown explicit EC groups in default builds. These versions are affected only when configured with the enable-ec_explicit_curves option.  The result is a hang that consumes a CPU core until the process is interrupted, a Denial of Service. The severity is Low because the attacker must supply the recipient certificate and the application must choose to encrypt to it; ordinary CMS encryption to trusted recipients is unaffected.  The fix makes EC key generation fail when the group has no valid nonzero private scalar, instead of retrying indefinitely.  FIPS impact: no The FIPS modules in 4.1, 4.0, 3.5 and 3.0 are not affected. The FIPS provider does not support arbitrary explicit EC domain parameters, so an order-1 group is rejected during key import before the vulnerable key generation path is reached."
              },
